## Method and reasoning
Start at the widest pair. If the left height is smaller, every narrower pair that still uses the same left endpoint is bounded by that height and a smaller width. Discard it. The symmetric rule applies to the right endpoint, including when heights tie. Use 64-bit area.

## Walk through the sample
The endpoints have height 3 and width 4, so they hold 12. Keeping either endpoint while narrowing the width caps the area below 12. Equal endpoint heights permit discarding either one.

## Complexity
Time O(n), auxiliary space O(1) besides the input.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (auto &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    long long best = 0;
    for (int l = 0, r = n - 1; l < r;) {
        best = max(best, (r - l) * min(a[l], a[r]));
        if (a[l] <= a[r])
            ++l;
        else
            --r;
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
~~~
